titre $ KEY TO KEYS of Pacific Northwest Key Council 
com$ adapt GF sept 2019
1,0$ Prepared for the Pacific Northwest Key CouncilBy Ian Gibson (South Vancouver Island Mycological Society) Nov. 2002 ///with minor revisions 2004, 2007, 2011, 2017, 2019   Copyright © 2002-2019 Pacific Northwest Key Council///This key is designed to allow people to find the appropriate Pacific Northwest Key Council key for an unknown mushroom, and to provide descriptions for those species that do not fall naturally into one of the keys.   

1,0$
1,101 $ a  gills are present under cap
1,2 $ b  gills are not present
2,3 $ a  well-defined cap and stem
2,11 $ b  no well-defined cap and stem
3,4 $ a  pores on underside of cap
3,5 $ b  no pores on underside of cap
4,0 $ a  tube layer that ends in pores strips easily from cap, fruitbody fleshy, usually on ground $ Boletes
4,0 $ b  tube layer does not strip easily from cap, texture usually tough and leathery, usually on wood $ Polypores
5,0 $ a  (3b)  teeth on underside of cap, fruitbody not jelly-like $ Toothed fungi (Hydnoid fungi)
5,6 $ b  no teeth on underside of cap or fruitbody jelly-like
6,0 $ a  veins on underside of cap as in chanterelles: thick, blunt, foldlike, shallow, may be forked or with cross veins, decurrent $ Veined fungi (Cantharelloid fungi)
6,7 $ b  no veins on underside of cap
7,0 $ a  fruitbodies grouped on dead Russulaceae mushrooms, cap whitish becoming powdery $ Lyophyllum and Allies (Asterophora lycoperdoides)
7,8 $ b  fruitbodies growing elsewhere, cap not whitish becoming powdery
8,0 $ a  fruitbody whitish, small with cap less than 1.5 cm wide so that gill-structure may not develop fully, slender stem centrally attached, spores inamyloid $ Mycenoid species (Hemimycena)
8,9 $ b  not whitish, or larger, or stem thick or lateral, or spores amyloid
9,0 $ a  fruitbody whitish, less than 3 cm tall, slender stem with fine hairs and attached at the side of cap that is kidney-shaped to asymmetrically funnel-shaped, margin often wavy, growing on mossy needle beds $ Stereopsis humphreyi*
9,10 $ b  not with the combination of features above
10,0 $ a  fruitbody whitish, kidney-shaped to spathulate or funnel-shaped, white cap less than 2.0 cm wide, slightly wrinkled or smooth spore bearing surface, short lateral stem, growth on moss $ Muscinupta laevis*
10,0 $ b  fruitbody not whitish or differently shaped or larger, or stem not lateral, or habitat different $ Veined fungi (Cantharelloid fungi)(a key where some mushrooms with smooth underside to cap are discussed, due to the species that vary from veined to smooth)
11,12 $ a  (2b)  growing on wood in form that is shelf-like, fan-like, or bracket-like, often but not always tough
11,13 $ b  not growing on wood, or form different; tough or not
12,0 $ a  pores on underside $ Polypores
12,0 $ b  teeth on underside $ Toothed fungi (Hydnoid fungi)
13,0 $ a  (11b)  growing underground $ Truffles & False Truffles
13,14 $ b  not growing underground
14,0 $ a  growing flat usually on wood but occasionally other fungi or other surfaces $ Crust fungi
14,15 $ b  not growing flat on wood
15,0 $ a  in the form of a nest less than 1 cm wide, often containing lens-shaped "eggs", or in form of 0.1-0.3 cm sphere shooting out spherical "egg" and leaving starlike rays $  [Nidulariaceae (Bird's Nest Fungi)]
15,16 $ b  not in the form of a nest, and not in form of sphere that leaves starlike rays
16,0 $ a  with foul-smelling slime at maturity, in the form of a club, sometimes with branches at the end, or in the form of a red sphere with a coarse netlike pattern $ [Club Fungi (Stinkhorns)]
16,17 $ b  not with foul-smelling slime, or not in the form of a club or netlike sphere
17,0 $ a  consistency like firm jelly (rubbery) $ Jelly fungi
17,18 $ b  consistency not jelly-like
18,0 $ a  cup-like or disc-like, or with tiny tubular fruitbody; with or without stem $ Cup fungi(key written only for Pezizales, not Leotiales or basidiomycete cups)
18,19 $ b  neither cup-like nor disc-like nor with tiny tubular fruitbody
19,20 $ a  more or less spherical, with spore mass inside that often becomes powdery, with stem or without stem
19,23 $ b  not spherical or not with spore mass inside that becomes powdery
20,0 $ a  at maturity outer layer splits into several starlike rays which curl back $ Puffballs without long stalks & Earthstars
20,21 $ b  not with outer layer splitting into starlike rays
21,0 $ a  long stem which is usually slender $ Long-stalked Puffballs (in Desert Fungi key)
21,22 $ b  stem short or absent
22,0 $ a  thick tough rigid skin when fresh, typically yellowish to brown but sometimes whitish, purplish, or blackish $ Earthballs (Sclerodermataceae)
22,0 $ b  skin not thick tough and rigid when fresh, often whitish when fresh but also other colors $ Puffballs without long stalks & Earthstars
23,0 $ a  (19b)  head of fungus convoluted or saddle-like but not honeycombed with large pits and ridges, and not with leaf-like lobes, usually with stem $ Morels, False Morels and Elfin Saddles (Helvellaceae)
23,24 $ b  head of fungus not convoluted or saddle-like, but may be honeycombed with large pits and ridges, or may have leaf-like lobes
24,0 $ a  head of fungus honeycombed with large pits and ridges, stem present $ Morels, False Morels and Elfin Saddles (Morchellaceae)
24,25 $ b.  head of fungus not honeycombed, stem present or absent
25,0 $ a  fleshy, unbranched upright club $ Club fungi
25,26 $ b  not in the form of fleshy unbranched upright club
26,27 $ a  fleshy, intricately branched or with leaflike lobes
26,28 $ b  not fleshy or neither intricately branched nor with leaflike lobes
27,0 $ a  medium to large, coral-like, profusely branched from common base, branches mostly erect, smooth, never ribbon-like, often brightly colored, spores usually ornamented, spore-bearing surface usually staining green or bluish with ferrous sulphate $ Coral fungi (Ramaria)
27,0 $ b  small to large, intricately branched or with leaf-like lobes, branches erect or not, smooth or rough, may be ribbon-like, color usually but not always whitish or grayish or yellowish, spores usually smooth, spore-bearing surface usually not staining green or bluish with ferrous sulphate $ [Coral fungi (other)]

28,0$ Not included in keys (microscopic species, lichens, slime moulds, moulds, mildews, rusts, smuts, Rhytisma, Taphrina, Hypocrea, Nectria, Hypoxylon, Daldinia, Dibotryon, Gymnosporangium, Pucciniastrum, etc.) $


101,102 $ a  stem off-center or absent
101,201 $ b  stem central
102,0 $ a  gill edge split lengthwise into two halves $ Schizophyllum*
102,103 $ b  gill edge not split lengthwise into two halves
103,105 $ a  spores decidedly pink, salmon-colored, reddish clay-colored, or brownish-pink
103,104 $ b  spores whitish, buff, slightly pinkish, clay-colored without reddish tones, brownish-yellow, olivaceous brown, lilac, violaceous, purple-brown, blackish brown or another brown
104,112 $ a  spores white, cream, cream-buff,  pale yellow, brownish-yellow, or slightly pinkish
104,107 $ b  spores buff, clay-colored without reddish tones, olivaceous-umber, lilac, violaceous, purple-brown, or blackish-brown, or another shade of brown
105,0 $ a  (103a)  cap densely tomentose, fruitbody entirely orange-yellow to orange $ Pleurotoid species (Phyllotopsis)
105,106 $ b  cap not densely tomentose, or fruitbody another color
106,0 $ a  cap 3-6 cm wide, deeply depressed becoming flat-depressed, hispid or nearly bald, white to cream or purplish with dark purple lines, spores dull brown to yellow-brown, or cinnamon-brown, sometimes with slight pinkish tint (and smooth under microscope) $ [Crepidotus]
106,0 $ b  fruitbody with a different combination of features, spores pink, salmon, or brownish-pink (and angular under microscope) $ Entolomataceae
107,0 $ a  (104b)  spores violaceous to lilac $ Pleurotoid species (Pleurotus)
107,108 $ b  spores not violaceous to lilac
108,0 $ a  gills readily removed from cap $ Gilled Boletes (Paxillus)
108,109 $ b  gills not readily removed from cap
109,0 $ a  spores reddish-brown or purplish to fuscous violet to dark violaceous-brown $ Deconica*
109,110 $ b  spores clay-colored, yellowish brown, cinnamon-brown, dull brown, light brown, olivaceous-umber or bright rusty-brown
110,0 $ a  fruitbody and lower stem coarsely scaly, dark brown to cinnamon or rusty-brown $ Phaeomarasmius*
110,111 $ b  fruitbody and lower stem not coarsely scaly, or not dark brown to cinnamon or rusty-brown
111,0 $ a  yellow-brown fruitbody, gills tinged yellowish or orange and often crimped, forked, and/or interveined, spores yellowish brown or light brown, stem lateral or absent $ Tapinella panuoides*
111,0 $ b  yellow-brown to red-brown fruitbody, gills cream to tan or yellow-brown, often forked and/or interveined, spores yellowish brown to brownish yellow, stem off-center to lateral, taste often bitter $ Tapinella atrotomentosa*
111,0 $ c  fruitbody whitish, ochraceous, yellowish, brownish, spores yellowish brown to cinnamon-brown or dull brown, stem lateral or absent $ [Crepidotus]
111,0 $ d  fruitbody everywhere olivaceous or olive-brown or spores dull brown or grayish-brown to umber, stem lateral or off-center $ [Simocybe]
112,0 $ a  (104a)  gills hard, regular to mazelike, fruitbody a bracket on wood $ Polypores
112,0 $ b  gills soft, regular to forking or anastomosing, yellow-tinged or orange-tinged, and often crimped, yellow-brown fruitbody on wood with lateral or absent stem $ Tapinella panuoides*
112,0 $ c  gills soft, regular to forking or anastomosing, cream to tan or yellow-brown, yellow-brown to red-brown fruitbody on wood with lateral or absent, velvety stem, taste often bitter $ Tapinella atrotomentosa*
112,113 $ d  gills soft, regular to forking or anastomosing, fruitbody not a bracket on wood
113,0 $ a  gills forking (dichotomously branching), fruitbody orange or brown, rarely whitish, mild odor, growing on ground $ Gilled boletes (Hygrophoropsis)
113,0 $ b  gills forking (dichotomously branching), fruitbody pink, fragrant odor of bubblegum, cinnamon candy, or grape soda, growing on ground $ Omphalinoid species (Aphroditeola olida)
113,114 $ c  gills either not strongly forking or fruitbody a different color or growing on wood
114,0 $ a  gills readily removed from cap $ Gilled Boletes (Paxillus)
114,115 $ b  gills not readily removed from cap
115,116 $ a  gills strongly anastomosing
115,118 $ b  gills not strongly anastomosing
116,0 $ a  fruitbody white, on grasses and Rubus canes $ Tetrapyrgos*
116,117 $ b  fruitbody a different color or habitat different
117,0 $ a  gills often veinlike as in chanterelles, fruitbody gray to gray-brown, among mosses $ [Omphalinoid species (Arrhenia)]
117,0 $ b  gills usually veinlike as in chanterelles, fruitbody reddish-brown to yellow-brown to tan, on hardwoods $ Veined Fungi (Plicaturopsis)
118,0 $ a  (115b)  gill edge with abundant large cells appearing ciliate under hand lens $ Tricholomopsis
118,119 $ b  gill edge not with abundant large cells appearing ciliate under hand lens
119,0 $ a  some part of fruitbody staining blackish, bluing, or turning red, (gills usually bluing with PDAB) $ Lyophyllum and Allies (Lyophyllum)
119,120 $ b  no part of fruitbody staining blackish, bluing, or turning red, (gills not usually bluing with PDAB)
120,0 $ a  cap up to 2cm wide, chalky whitish, dry, minutely hairy, without stem or with short lateral whitish stem, growth on wood, spore deposit white, (spores 5-6 x 4.5-5.5 um) $ Pleurotoid species (Cheimonophyllum candidissimum)
120,121 $ b  not with this combination of features
121,0 $ a  cap flesh and flesh of gills (at times) either completely gelatinous or with gelatinous layers, gill edge not serrate under hand lens $ Pleurotoid species
121,122 $ b  cap and gills neither gelatinous nor with gelatinous layers, gill edge smooth to serrate under hand lens
122,0 $ a  gill edge not serrate and cap fleshy $ Pleurotoid species
122,123 $ b  gill edge serrate or cap tough and leathery to corky
123,0 $ a  gill edge not serrate $ Pleurotoid species
123,124 $ b  gill edge serrate
124,0 $ a  gill edge coarsely toothed, cap whitish-ocher to brown, stem usually central $ Lentinellus & Neolentinus (Neolentinus)
124,125 $ b  gill edge finely toothed, cap brown, ocher, or slightly violaceous, stem central to lateral
125,0 $ a  fruitbodies with one of following features a) cap hairy tomentose and stem base strigose with gray or greenish hairs, b) cap with small blackish fine scales on a white background and stem blackish scaly, c) cap bald when young and orange-yellow, ocher-yellow, or ocher-brown, or d) gills ocher to pinkish brown or violaceous $ Pleurotoid species (Panus)
125,0 $ b  fruitbodies without one of the above features, cap variable in color but usually brown, rarely whitish, gills usually serrate and white, whitish-yellow or pallid-pinkish $ Lentinellus & Neolentinus (Lentinellus)
201,0 $ a  gills and/or cap turning to inky blackish liquid $ Coprinoid Species
201,202 $ b  gills and/or cap not turning to inky blackish liquid
